    "description": "CustomerProfileService, RecommendationService, and PermissionService run inside the same WebRequestSession and all need the Customer, Address, and Subscription records touched by the request. The current data loader performs an independent SELECT for every lookup, so the profile path and permission path can each create a separate Customer instance for the same customer_id. The profile path renames the customer, while the permission path compares a locally cached address and later writes its older copy, silently replacing the new value. Repeated access also causes avoidable database traffic. The shared session must keep one authoritative in-memory instance per row for the duration of the request, including objects first reached through different queries, while still allowing the loader to retrieve related addresses only when a caller actually reads them. Session completion must discard the registry so the next request does not reuse stale state, and objects used by concurrent requests must never be shared across session boundaries.",
    "decision_statement": "Whether WebRequestSession should return the same in-memory customer, address, and subscription instances on repeated and cross-service lookups within one request.",
    "notes": "identity-map fits the one-authoritative-instance-per-row registry within WebRequestSession; lazy-load covers deferred association fetching and unit-of-work supports coordinated writes."
  },
